# readings.ezr — a summary over a list of sensor readings.
#
#   cd 5-runtime-java && ./ezr ../examples/readings.ezr
#
# NOT ezrun.py, and not `ever run`: this file is written in the forge's
# core (CORE.md), where `let x = v in body` is an expression. v4.10's
# grammar has `let x = v` as a statement instead, so it refuses this file
# at the first `let` in `biggest`. (No line number here on purpose: it is
# a number about this file, written in this file, and every edit to the
# comment moves it. It was wrong twice before this sentence replaced it.)
# readings.ever is the same program in that grammar. Which of
# the two lineages the project keeps is an open question -- see CORE.md
# and FINDINGS.md section 7.4.
#
# Two things worth watching.
#
# Every line `show` prints carries its own confidence, because in EZR
# confidence is part of the value rather than something a logger adds.
# The count reads 256/256 -- `len` is a builtin, and there is nothing
# uncertain about counting. Everything after it reads 120/256, because
# it came through `total`, `biggest` or `smallest`, and no evidence has
# been offered for any of them. [DEF] enters a definition below the
# execute floor: written is not verified. Supply Examples and the same
# numbers come back worth more:
#
#   --call 'total([12, 7, 19])'                        38 @ 120/256
#   ... -x 'total([]) = 0' -x 'total([5]) = 5' \
#       -x 'total([1, 2]) = 3'                         38 @ 217/256
#
# Same 38. Three witnesses' worth more of it.
#
# (These read 256/256 when this file was written, before [APP]'s c_f
# term was implemented. That was the defect, not the documentation.)
#
# And `summary` never checks for an empty list. It does not need to. The
# lets run in order, so the mean is reached first and 0 / 0 refuses there:
#
#   --call 'summary([])'   Z(misbound) - division by zero     exit 1
#   --call 'biggest([])'   Z(unbound) - tail of an empty list exit 1
#   --call 'total([])'     0  @ 120/256                       exit 0
#
# Three different answers to "what about nothing", each correct. The sum
# of no readings really is 0. The largest of none is not a number, and
# EZR says so rather than crashing or quietly handing back a zero that
# would then be averaged into somebody's report.

def total(xs) = if len(xs) == 0 then 0
                else head(xs) + total(tail(xs))

def biggest(xs) = if len(xs) == 1 then head(xs)
                  else let rest = biggest(tail(xs)) in
                       if head(xs) > rest then head(xs) else rest

def smallest(xs) = if len(xs) == 1 then head(xs)
                   else let rest = smallest(tail(xs)) in
                        if head(xs) < rest then head(xs) else rest

# `let` earns its keep here: the count and the sum are each computed once
# and then reused for the mean, rather than walking the list a third time.
def summary(xs) = let n  = show(len(xs))      in
                  let s  = show(total(xs))    in
                  let m  = show(s / n)        in
                  let hi = show(biggest(xs))  in
                  let lo = show(smallest(xs)) in
                  hi - lo

def main() = summary([12, 7, 19, 3, 14, 8])
